  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <para>For 1 - 3, determine whether each ordered pair is a solution to the inequality \(y &gt; x − 1\).</para>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="_4f57fb23-4a13-46c7-bade-4751d590d15b" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> \((0,1)\) </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Yes</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.11.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is Yes. Substitute the ordered pair \((0, 1)\) into the inequality \(y &gt; x − 1\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution.
        \(1 &gt; 0 − 1\)
        \(1 &gt; 0 − 1\) \(1 &gt; − 1\) \(1 &gt; − 1\) 1 is greater than -1. Yes, it is a solution because the inequality is true. 1 is greater than -1. Yes, it is a solution because the inequality is true.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_2e0361f0-9152-4dfd-91ed-b78c12827035" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>\((−4,−1)\) </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Yes.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.11.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is Yes. Substitute the ordered pair \((−4,−1)\) into the inequality \(y &gt; x − 1\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution.
        \(-1 &gt; -4 − 1\)
        \(-1 &gt; -4 − 1\) \(-1 &gt; −5\) \(-1 &gt; −5\) -1 is greater than -5. Yes, it is a solution because the inequality is true.
       -1 is greater than -5. Yes, it is a solution because the inequality is true.
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="b0de4861-3d99-462f-b776-39e212e0fc77" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> \((4,2)\) </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! No.</para>
            <para>Try again. Revist activity 2.11.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is No. Substitute the ordered pair \((4, 2)\) into the inequality \(y &gt; x − 1\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution. 
        \(2 &gt; 4 − 1\)
        \(2 &gt; 4 − 1\) \(2 &gt; 3\) \(2 &gt; 3\) 2 is not greater than 3. No, it is not a solution because the inequality is not true. 2 is not greater than 3. No, it is not a solution because the inequality is not true.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_7b40b269-3d20-4270-a430-706c08eb23ab" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> \((0,0)\) </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Yes, \((0,0)\) is a solution to the inequality.</para>
            <para>Try again. Remember to substitute the coordinates into the inequality to check if it holds true.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is Yes. Substitute the ordered pair \((0, 0)\) into the inequality \(y &gt; x - 3\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement. 
        \(0 &gt; 0 -3\)
        \(0 &gt; 0 -3\) \(0 &gt; -3\) \(0 &gt; -3\) 0 is indeed greater than -3, making it a solution to the inequality. 0 is indeed greater than -3, making it a solution to the inequality.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_1fc70666-6c63-4cc0-8df5-53817254d3b5" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> \((2,1)\) </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! No, \((2,1)\) is not a solution to the inequality.</para>
            <para>Try again. Check the inequality with the given coordinates.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is No. Substitute the ordered pair \((2, 1)\) into the inequality \(y &gt; x - 3\) to determine if it's a solution. 
        \(1 &gt; 2 -3\)
        \(1 &gt; 2 -3\) \(1 &gt; -1\) \(1 &gt; -1\) Although 1 is greater than -1, the coordinates \((2,1)\) do not satisfy the original inequality \(y &gt; x - 3\), therefore it is not a solution. Although 1 is greater than -1, the coordinates \((2,1)\) do not satisfy the original inequality \(y &gt; x - 3\), therefore it is not a solution.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_6a8c18e6-92fc-43ad-ba1e-d1351ac96415" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> \((−1,−5)\) </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! No</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.11.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is No. Substitute the ordered pair \((-1,-5)\) into the inequality \(y &gt; x − 3\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution. 
        \(-5 &gt; -1 −3\)
        \(-5 &gt; -1 −3\)
        \(-5 &gt; -4\)
        \(-5 &gt; -4\)
        -5 is not greater than -4. No, it is not a solution because the inequality is not true.
        -5 is not greater than -4. No, it is not a solution because the inequality is not true.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="f12e8b48-6d40-47d3-ae44-2f85f62d6c21" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> \((1,1)\) </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Yes</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.11.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is Yes. Substitute the ordered pair \((1, 1)\) into the inequality \(2x+3y&gt;2\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution.
        \(2(1)+3(1) &gt; 2\)
        \(2(1)+3(1) &gt; 2\)
        \(5&gt;2\)
        \(5&gt;2\)
        5 is greater than 2. Yes, it is a solution because the inequality is true.
        5 is greater than 2. Yes, it is a solution because the inequality is true.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_2c7ac670-7c24-412c-ade2-c8818efc0611" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> \((4,−3)\) </para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! No</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.11.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is No. Substitute the ordered pair \((4,-3)\) into the inequality \(2x+3y&gt;2\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution. 
        \(2(4)+3(-3) &gt; 2\)
        \(2(4)+3(-3) &gt; 2\)
        \(8-9 &gt; 2\)
        \(8-9 &gt; 2\)
        \(-1 &gt; 2\)
        \(-1 &gt; 2\)
        -1 is not greater than 2. No, it is not a solution because the inequality is not true.
        -1 is not greater than 2. No, it is not a solution because the inequality is not true.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--retries set to 2 in this section-->
      <!--BEGIN PRACTICE PROBLEM MULTIPLE CHOICE -->
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="ded25a4b-2d81-4d4d-9bff-397f05af0ea0" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Which inequality represents the graph?</para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="graph of y &lt; 3x − 4">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_13_10_01.jpg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(y\;\leq\;3x\;-\;4\)</para>
            <para>\(y &lt; 3x − 4\)</para>
            <para>\(y &gt; 3x − 4\)</para>
            <para>\(y\;\geq\;3x\;-\;4\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(y &lt; 3x − 4\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.11.3. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(y &lt; 3x − 4\). The graph has a dotted line, so any answers that have \(\geq\) or\(\leq\) can be eliminated. 
        The two remaining options are \(y&gt;3x−4\) and \(y&gt;3x−4\).
        The two remaining options are \(y&gt;3x−4\) and \(y&gt;3x−4\).
        If we set \(x\) to 0, we can see that the \(y\)-coordinate is -4. Look at the shading in the graph to determine if y&gt;-4 or \(y&gt;-4\). The graph is shaded to the right and down so \(y&gt;-4\). Therefore the equation \(y&gt;3x−4\) is the correct answer.
        If we set \(x\) to 0, we can see that the \(y\)-coordinate is -4. Look at the shading in the graph to determine if y&gt;-4 or \(y&gt;-4\). The graph is shaded to the right and down so \(y&gt;-4\). Therefore the equation \(y&gt;3x−4\) is the correct answer.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--END PRACTICE PROBLEM MULTIPLE CHOICE -->
      <para>For questions 10 and 11, use this graph of the equation \(2y−x=1\) as a guide to answer the questions.</para>
      <figure>
        <media alt="Graph of a line.">
          <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_13_10_02.svg"/>
        </media>
      </figure>
      <!--Retries set to 1 in this section-->
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="_7f152e76-fb43-4be0-8bfa-4843d43903b0" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Is the point \((0, \frac12)\) a solution to the equation?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Yes</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.11.3. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is Yes. Substitute the ordered pair \((0, \frac{1}{2})\) into the equation \(2y−x=1\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution. 
        \(2(\frac{1}{2})−0=1\)
        \(2(\frac{1}{2})−0=1\)\(1-0=1\)\(1-0=1\)\(1=1\)\(1=1\) 1 equals 1. Yes, it is a solution because the equation is true.
       1 equals 1. Yes, it is a solution because the equation is true.
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="efb0a1f2-d550-4912-bfe9-35a088fe95d5" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Is the point \((0,2)\) a solution to the inequality \(2y −
          x &gt; 1\)?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! Yes</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.11.3. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is Yes. Substitute the ordered pair \((0, 2)\) into the inequality \(2y−x&gt;1\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution. 
        \(2(2)−0&gt;1\)
        \(2(2)−0&gt;1\)
        \(4-0&gt;1\)
        \(4-0&gt;1\)
        \(4&gt;1\)
        \(4&gt;1\)
        4 is greater than 1. Yes, it is a solution because the inequality is true.
      
        4 is greater than 1. Yes, it is a solution because the inequality is true.
      </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Retries set tp 1 this section-->
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="_04793c5c-6570-44f1-9a26-ee459ae51b57" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>Consider the linear equation \(2y − 3x = 5\). The pair \((-1,1)\) is a solution to the equation. Which of
          the following is also a solution to the equation?</para>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\((1, 5)\)</para>
            <para>\((3, 7)\)</para>
            <para>\((5, 11)\)</para>
            <para>\((-3, -1)\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! (3, 7)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revisit activity 2.11.2. </para>
            <para>The correct answer is (3, 7).
      </para>
            <para>Substitute the ordered pair \((3, 7)\) into the equation \(2y−3x=5\) to determine if the coordinates make it a true statement, which makes it a solution. 
        \(2(7)−3(3)=5\)
        \(2(7)−3(3)=5\)
        \(14-9=5\)
        \(14-9=5\)
        \(5=5\)
        \(5=5\)
        5 equals 5. The ordered pair (3, 7) is a solution because the equation is true.
        5 equals 5. The ordered pair (3, 7) is a solution because the equation is true.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_6bb26149-83d5-417f-be34-7ca51c59d2bd" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para>The boundary line on the graph represents the equation \( 5x + 2y = 6\). Write an inequality that is represented by the graph.</para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="graph with boundary line that represents the equation 5x + 2y = 6">
                <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_13_10_03.svg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
            <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para>\(5x + 2y &lt; -6\)</para>
            <para>\(-5x - 2y &lt; 6\)</para>
            <para>\(5x + 2y &lt; 2\)</para>
            <para>\(5x + 2y &lt; 6\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! \(5x + 2y &lt; 6\)</para>
            <para>Try again. Revist activity 2.11.4.</para>
            <para>The correct answer is \(5x + 2y &lt; 6\). The graph is shaded to the left and down and it crosses the \(y\)-axis at 3, so the solution has to show the
        \(y&gt;3\) when \(x=0\).
        If we plug in 0 into the inequality \(5x+2y&gt;6\)
        If we plug in 0 into the inequality \(5x+2y&gt;6\)
        \(5(0)+2y&gt;6\)
        \(5(0)+2y&gt;6\)
        \(2y&gt;6\)
        \(2y&gt;6\)
        \(y&gt;3\)
        \(y&gt;3\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
    </section>
